\begin{proof}
Let $\operatorname{LocNilp}(A)=\bigcap\{P\triangleleft A:P\text{ prime},\ \operatorname{LocNilp}(A/P)=0\}$. 
We have $\operatorname{LocNilp}(A)\subseteq\bigcap\{P\}$ because if $\operatorname{LocNilp}(A/P)=0$, then $P$ contains $\operatorname{LocNilp}(A)$. 
For the reverse inclusion, let $a\in A\setminus\operatorname{LocNilp}(A)$. 
We show there exists a prime ideal $P$ of $A$ with $\operatorname{LocNilp}(A/P)=0$ and $a\notin P$.

Since $a\notin\operatorname{LocNilp}(A)$, the ideal $\langle a\rangle$ is not locally nilpotent. 
Thus there exists a finitely generated subalgebra $S\subseteq\langle a\rangle$ which is not nilpotent. 
Consider
\[
M=\{I\triangleleft A:a\notin I\text{ and }S^n\not\subseteq I,\ \forall n\geq1\}.
\]
$M$ is nonempty since $(0)\in M$ (as $a\neq0$ and $S$ is not nilpotent). 
Moreover, $M$ is inductive: if $I_1\subseteq I_2\subseteq\cdots$ is a chain in $M$, set $I=\bigcup I_n$. 
If $I\notin M$, then there exists $n\geq1$ such that $S^n\subseteq I$. 
Let $S_0=\{v:v\text{ is a product of generators of }S\text{ with }n\leq d(v)\leq2n\}$, where $d(v)$ is the number of generators in $v$. 
Since $S_0\subseteq I$, there exists $m\geq1$ with $S_0\subseteq I_m$. 
We prove $S^n\subseteq I_m$ by induction on the degree of products. 
If $u\in S^n$ with $d(u)\leq2n$, then $u\in S_0\subseteq I_m$. 
If $d(u)>2n$, write $u=\sum_i v_i w_i$ with $d(v_i)+d(w_i)=d(u)>2n$. 
Then $n<d(v_i)$ (or $d(w_i)$), so by induction $v_i\in I_m$ and similarly $w_i\in I_m$, hence $u\in I_m$. 
Thus $S^n\subseteq I_m$, contradicting
